Tournament Analysis over proof lenses
  observable=(preserves_bound, exact_audit, LRC_remaining_gap, sign_structure, locality)
  switch/gauge: generated words and signs are retained before scalarizing
  Hamiltonian path=['generated_depth_occupancy', 'signed_delsarte_dual', 'delsarte_quasicode_parity', 'halfarc_sign_orbit', 'weak_regular_equitable_quotient', 'dessin_belyi_passport', 'sparse_tanner_expander', 'unit_distance_spectral_transfer']
  score histogram={'generated_depth_occupancy': 7, 'signed_delsarte_dual': 6, 'delsarte_quasicode_parity': 5, 'halfarc_sign_orbit': 4, 'weak_regular_equitable_quotient': 3, 'dessin_belyi_passport': 2, 'sparse_tanner_expander': 1, 'unit_distance_spectral_transfer': 0}
  directed_3cycles=0 (ties only; strict tie-break gives 0)
==============================================================================
Conclusion
  The Tanner graph exists but is not weakly regular or LDPC-like.
  Its useful invariant is the signed half-arc orientation: support
  automorphisms never mix + and - edge orbits.  A Belyi/dessin passport
  is a good address layer for the carrier, but only the signed passport
  remembers the Delsarte dominance certificate.  The proof route should
  stay: generated depth word -> signed Delsarte/quasicode parity -> cap,
  while using weak-regular/unit-distance/Tanner analogies as audits.
